---
/**
 * Gold, then bitcoin, then the tax on both and the funds that hold them. One line each
 * on commodities and ether. Fees are read from the shelf; the two headline measurements
 * from figure records.
 */
import Button from "~/components/Button.astro";
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import StatGrid from "~/components/StatGrid.astro";
import Verdict from "~/components/Verdict.astro";
import { fundByTicker } from "~/content/shelf";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import { figureValue } from "~/lib/figures";

const fee = (ticker: string): string => {
  const bp = fundByTicker(ticker).expenseRatioBp;
  if (bp === null) throw new Error(`${ticker} has no fee on the shelf`);
  return `${(bp / 100).toFixed(2)}%`;
};

const STATS = [
  { value: await figureValue("gold-over-cash"), label: "Gold's return a year over cash", note: "1975 to mid-2026, after storage costs" },
  { value: "16%", label: "Gold's swings a year", note: "Against none for cash, which did the same job in a fall" },
  { value: "60%", label: "Bitcoin's return a year over cash", note: "2015 to mid-2026, with swings of 71% a year" },
  {
    value: await figureValue("bitcoin-drawdown-deepened"),
    label: "A portfolio's worst fall with 10% bitcoin",
    note: "2015 to 2026. The only holding tested that deepened the fall at every weight",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Gold and bitcoin"
  description="Gold does the job of cash in a crash, with big swings, and adds no return the tests can see: optional, small, in a retirement account. Bitcoin is the only holding tested that made the worst fall deeper at every weight: at most 2% as a bet, and zero is fine."
  xl
>
  <Hero
    title="Gold and bitcoin"
    lede="Gold does the job of cash in a crash, with big swings instead of none, and adds no return the tests can see. Bitcoin is the only holding tested that made the portfolio's worst fall deeper at every weight."
  >
    <p class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-ink-2">
      <Verdict word="Too close to call" gloss="Gold: optional, at most 5%, only in place of cash or bonds." />
      <Verdict word="No" gloss="Bitcoin, as protection: at most 1% to 2% as a bet, and zero is fine." />
    </p>
  </Hero>

  <StatGrid items={STATS} class="wide mt-10" />

  <div class="prose mt-10">
    <h2 id="gold">Gold</h2>
    <p>Since 1975, the first year Americans could own it freely, gold earned
    <Figure id="gold-over-cash" inline /> after a 0.25% storage cost, with swings of 16% a year and a worst
    fall of 91% behind cash from its peak. In the worst tenth of stock months since 1975, moving 10% of stocks
    into gold added 0.92% back in the average such month, against 0.80% for cash-like government bills on the
    same months; gold rose in 56% of them. So gold does what cash does in a fall, and charges you 16% a year of
    swings for it.</p>
    <p>It has a record against inflation that cash lacks: gold rose 90% through the late-1970s inflation while
    long government bonds lost 40%, gained 22% through 2008 and 2009, and lost 7% in the 2022 rate shock. How
    you pay for it decides the sign: bought by selling 10% of stocks, gold cost the portfolio 0.4% a year; added
    on top through a fund that borrows, it added 0.2%. Neither can be told from zero.</p>
    <p>Its price after inflation, $4,027 an ounce in July 2026 money, is higher than in 98.5% of months since
    1975 and above both the January 1980 and August 2011 peaks in the same money. I would not open a gold
    position at this price. If you hold some, hold at most 5%, in place of cash or bonds and never in place of
    stocks, in a retirement account.</p>

    <h2 id="bitcoin">Bitcoin</h2>
    <p>Between 2015 and mid-2026 bitcoin earned 60% a year over cash with swings of 71% a year and a worst fall
    of 76%: the best return for its risk of anything measured on this site. Adding it still made things worse,
    because it falls when stocks fall, and harder: about one and a half times as far in both directions, with a
    link to stocks that has tightened to 0.53 over the last seven years. It rose in 2 of the 12 worst stock
    months of the past decade.</p>
    <p>A 10% position took a portfolio's worst fall from <Figure id="bitcoin-drawdown-deepened" inline showUnit={false} />
    over 2015 to 2026. No other holding tested did that at every weight. In 2022 it fell 58% while stocks fell
    25%; between its October 2025 high and June 2026 it fell 53% while the S&amp;P 500's own fall was 9%. Over
    the five years to August 2026 it returned 56% against 73% for the S&amp;P 500, with about four times the
    swings, and 137 months of its best-ever returns cannot distinguish it from the S&amp;P 500 at matched
    risk.</p>
    <p>A fund that holds bitcoin on top of stocks by borrowing beat the same bitcoin bought by selling stocks by
    about 0.4% a year, and that gain is only the stock return on the money not sold. The borrowed bitcoin part
    was negative in 12 of the 13 worst stock months.</p>
    <p>BlackRock's own guidance for the fund it sells is 1% to 2% with a 2% cap; Vanguard recommends none. The
    verdict rests on 13 bad stock months since 2015, so read "No" as "no evidence it helps", and not as proof it
    hurts. A stock bear market in which bitcoin does not fall harder than stocks would reopen it.</p>

    <h2 id="tax-and-funds">Tax, and which fund</h2>
    <p>Both are taxed badly in an ordinary brokerage account. Gold held through a fund that owns bars is a
    collectible: long-term gains are taxed at up to 28% plus a 3.8% surtax, against 23.8% on stocks, so gold
    goes in a retirement account, where the rate does not apply. A fund that borrows to hold gold on top of
    stocks (GDE) avoids the collectible rate but paid out income taxed like wages worth 1.53% a year. Spot
    bitcoin funds are trusts whose gains flow through as if you owned the coin; hold bitcoin in a taxable
    account, where a 75% fall at least gives a usable loss.</p>
    <p>The gold funds hold the same bars in a vault and differ only in fee: IAUM {fee("IAUM")}, GLDM {fee("GLDM")},
    IAU {fee("IAU")}, GLD {fee("GLD")}. The bitcoin funds hold the same coins: Grayscale's mini trust (BTC) {fee("BTC")},
    BITB {fee("BITB")}, IBIT and FBTC {fee("IBIT")}. Avoid crypto "income" funds that pay 27% to 73% a year in
    distributions out of the position itself.</p>

    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>Gold: none, or at most 5% in place of cash or bonds, the cheapest fund, in a retirement account.
      Bitcoin: none, or at most 2% in a taxable account, labelled speculation in your own notes so you do not
      mistake it for protection.</p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="the-rest">Commodities and other coins</h2>
    <p>Broad commodity funds (PDBC, DBC) protect against inflation and not against a crash: they gained 144%
    across 1973 and 1974, but in the worst tenth of stock months since 1926 they rose in only 36% of them,
    because a recession that sinks stocks sinks demand for oil and copper too. Ether pays about 1.3% to 1.9% a
    year for staking before a fee that can exceed it, on an asset that fell 67% from its 2025 high.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <Button href="/strategies/crash-protection/">What actually protects you</Button>
    <Button href="/strategies/" variant="outline">Every other idea</Button>
  </div>
</Base>
